      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. First, the red team: see what Quayholt's staff have put online, then ring the IT service desk as Gordon McLeish, a locked-out claims manager, and try to talk Leila Nassar into a password reset. Then swap seats: you're Leila, taking three calls and an overnight sign-in prompt. Only Leila's seat is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now you're the one answering",
        "flipIntro": "This is your call as Leila heard it on the service desk. The lines picked out below are what made it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Mid-morning, more calls holding, Leila picked this one up. The caller had Gordon's name, team, manager and staff number, every answer right, so she reached for the reset. He'd done his homework, and the callback was the one thing that would have told him from the real Gordon.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your call didn't get through this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of call comes in to the service desk, and you're the one answering.",
        "flipMissed": "Your call didn't get what it was after this time, so there's nothing of yours to replay. Next, the same kind of call comes in to the service desk, and you're the one answering.",
        "noBlameLine": "Nothing here counts against you. These callers rehearse sounding right, and a service desk that tries to help them is doing its job under pressure.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: a name, a team, a manager and a staff number can all be found online, so on a phone call they prove nothing. Before you reset a password or move someone's sign-in prompts, hang up and ring them back on the number already on file, not one they give you. If a sign-in prompt arrives that you didn't start, deny it and report it using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These calls were made by people whose job is to sound right, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "flipCallIntro": "This is your call to the service desk, as Leila heard it. The lines picked out below are what would have caught it.",
        "defendIntro": "Now you're in Leila's seat. You started at 07:00 and it's mid-morning: three calls are waiting and a sign-in prompt from overnight is still on your phone. Take them one at a time.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "Why the call and the prompts worked",
          "text": "The caller had Gordon's name, team, manager and staff number, and every one was findable online, so none of them proved who was on the line. Only a callback to the number already on file could tell the real Gordon from the imposter.\n\nThe sign-in prompts that followed are the same attack from another angle. Someone with the password fires off one approval request after another, counting on a tired person tapping approve just to stop the buzzing. A prompt you didn't start is never one to approve, however many times it comes."
        }
      },
